title: Cluster-scope RBAC discovery sends SelfSubjectRulesReview with an empty namespace,
  which the API server rejects
updated: 2026-10-19T21:20:48Z
---

`Permissions::discover` adds a review with `spec.namespace => ''` 'for cluster-scoped resources' (lib/MCP/K8s/Permissions.pm:131-135) and swallows its failure (`eval`, no warn). kube-apiserver answers a SelfSubjectRulesReview without namespace with 400 'no namespace on request' (pkg/registry/authorization/selfsubjectrulesreview/rest.go) - the review is namespace-scoped by design. If that holds, `$rules{''}` never exists on a real cluster, and every `can_do($verb, $plural, '')` is false.

Consequences in lib/MCP/K8s.pm: with more than one reachable namespace `_resolve_namespace` returns undef (line 540-547), every tool then checks `can_do(..., $ns // '')` (e.g. 986, 1046, 1369) and answers 'Permission denied: cannot list Node' - also for a cluster-admin, and also for a plain `k8s_list resource=Pod` without namespace, where the right answer would be 'namespace required'. Cluster-scoped kinds (Node, Namespace, PersistentVolume, ClusterRole, CRDs) are only reachable if the LLM guesses to pass an arbitrary namespace. The 'Cluster-scoped' block of `summary` never appears. The t/05 mock (t/05-server-tools.t:176) answers the '' review with rules, which is why the suite does not see it.

Not verified against a live API server in this review - check first with `kubectl create --raw /apis/authorization.k8s.io/v1/selfsubjectrulesreviews -f -` and an empty namespace.

Done when: cluster-scoped permissions come from something the API actually answers (the namespaced reviews already contain ClusterRoleBinding-granted rules; or SelfSubjectAccessReview per call), a missing namespace on a namespaced kind yields a 'namespace required' message instead of 'Permission denied', and the mock mirrors the 400.

